== Cognition / process audit ==

[[Cognition]] remains a broad family of processes rather than a synonym for consciousness, intelligence, or neural activity.

[[Perception]] separates sensory input from perceptual organization while acknowledging that the biological boundary is not a clean serial stage division.

[[Attention]] separates selection from awareness and from [[Salience]].

[[Salience]] distinguishes perceptual prominence, motivational significance, and broader priority without reducing any of them to attention itself.

== Memory / working-memory audit ==

[[Memory]] is explicitly reconstructive and multi-system; retrieval is not described as literal playback.

[[Working memory]] is model-plural:

* Baddeley's multicomponent framework is presented as influential rather than definitive;
* Cowan/embedded-processes and other attention/interference/resource approaches remain visible;
* no fixed universal item count is canonized;
* working memory is not equated with short-term memory.

== Learning / reasoning / metacognition audit ==

[[Learning]] stays biological/cognitive and explicitly distinguishes [[Machine learning]].

[[Reasoning]] stays broader than formal logic and interfaces cleanly with future Wave 5 deductive/inductive/abductive descendants.

[[Metacognition]] separates monitoring from control and separates confidence from accuracy.

[[Cognitive load]] distinguishes working-memory demands from task difficulty and treats intrinsic/extraneous/germane terminology as theory/history-sensitive rather than timeless taxonomy.

== Cognitive-bias audit ==

[[Cognitive bias]] requires an explicit normative/statistical/task benchmark.

The wave does not equate heuristic use with irrationality.

[[Confirmation bias]] is treated as a family of search, testing, interpretation, weighting, and memory effects rather than a synonym for deliberate cherry-picking.

[[Anchoring effect]] is not reduced to insufficient adjustment; multiple mechanisms remain explicit.

[[Availability heuristic]] concerns retrieval/generation accessibility and is not reduced to recency or salience.

[[Framing effect]] distinguishes risky-choice, attribute, and goal framing and acknowledges pragmatic meaning differences.

[[Selection bias]] is explicitly excluded from the cognitive-bias ontology and uses Science/Mathematics Domains.

== Priming audit ==

[[Priming]] distinguishes:

* repetition priming;
* perceptual priming;
* semantic priming;
* broader social/behavioral priming claims.

The robust existence of classic cognitive priming effects is not used as evidence for behaviorally distant priming claims.

The pages do not imply that priming establishes arbitrary subliminal control or persuasion.

== Social-cognition audit ==

[[Anthropomorphism]] does not define every humanlike attribution as false. Comparative evidence determines whether a specific attribution is justified.

[[Theory of mind]]:

* is not a literal explicit theory requirement;
* is not reduced to false-belief tasks;
* acknowledges language, attention, memory, inhibition, and response-selection demands;
* treats infant implicit-task interpretations as contested;
* treats AI/LLM task success as behavioral evidence rather than proof of humanlike mechanism.

== Distributed / embodied cognition audit ==

[[Distributed cognition]] uses a functional system boundary across people/artifacts/representations and explicitly rejects equivalence with distributed computing or a mystical group mind.

[[Embodied cognition]] is presented as a family of approaches.

Grounded cognition, enactivism, situated cognition, extended cognition, and embodied cognition are not collapsed into synonyms.

The two concepts can overlap but remain distinct:

<pre>
distributed cognition → unit-of-analysis distribution
embodied cognition    → bodily/sensorimotor constitution
</pre>

== Thought-suppression / ironic-process audit ==

This is the principal evidence-sensitive correction in Wave 4.

[[Thought suppression]] does '''not''' claim:

<pre>
attempt suppression → inevitable rebound
</pre>

It distinguishes:

* immediate suppression effectiveness;
* post-suppression rebound;
* cognitive-load effects;
* white-bear paradigms;
* think/no-think memory-suppression paradigms;
* adaptiveness from effectiveness.

Contemporary review evidence questioning universal paradoxical effects is preserved alongside the classic Wegner literature.

[[Ironic process theory]] is assigned '''Supported''' rather than Established and is described as a specific theory of mental control, not as the definition of thought suppression or a universal cognitive law.

Its strongest scientific use is where it generates discriminating predictions such as increased target accessibility under cognitive load.

== "Pink elephant bias" audit ==

No canonical page was created.

The phrase is treated as an informal/popular description of thought-suppression difficulty rather than an established scientific construct.

Future aliasing, if useful for search, should point to [[Thought suppression]] rather than create a separate semantic identity.

